    // ----- Generators -----
    // A generator body is an ORDINARY IR function; what makes it a generator is that the
    // closure is handed to js_genfn, so calling the result creates a generator object over
    // it instead of running it. The body then runs on its own goroutine on the host side,
    // and 'yield' is a handshake with the pending next() (see abnf/jsrt.go). The upshot for
    // the compiler is that a generator needs no code transformation at all: 'yield' is one
    // more external call, and every statement form works inside a generator unchanged.
    // A generator or async body runs at its first RESUMPTION, not at the call, so it
    // cannot take its receiver from js_this at entry: by then js_this answers whatever
    // call resumed it (natively the generator object behind next(), under llvm.Run
    // nothing). What the program calls is therefore a thin BINDER that takes `this` at
    // the call exactly as an ordinary function does (OrdinaryCallBindThis, strict or
    // sloppy) and builds the coroutine over a body closure whose environment is the
    // binder's scope; the body is emitted without a `this` of its own (lexThis) and
    // finds this one. A class INSTANCE method needs no binder: its receiver travels
    // in the argument array.
    function coroOf(b, idx, kind, sc) {
