    // delegate's RETURN value - the `value` of the record whose `done` ended the loop -
    // and undefined for an array or a string, which have no return value to give.
    //
    // Inside an async generator each step of the delegate is AWAITED (emitAwait below),
    // which is what makes 'yield* otherAsyncGen()' terminate: an async generator's next()
    // answers a PROMISE, whose `done` is undefined, so driving it unawaited spins forever.
    //
    // THE RESUME DISPOSITION, and why it is not a stack. The loop keeps what the driver
    // last sent in - RAW, in the scope slot 0ysent - and branches on its SHAPE at the top
    // of every turn. A normal resume drives the delegate's next() WITH the sent value
    // (the specification's `received.value`, which is how `g.next(v)` reaches a delegated
    // generator at all). A {__athrow, v} record - the one ag.throw(e) puts on the resume
    // channel, and the one emitYieldStarCatch rebuilds from the value a plain g.throw(v)
    // RAISES at this yield - drives the delegate's THROW() instead, so a delegate that
    // would catch the throw does, and the yield* carries on with what it yields next. A
    // delegate with no throw method is CLOSED through its return() and the yield* raises
    // a TypeError, which is what node does for `yield* [1,2]`.
    //
    // Both arms are driven IN FRAME. The await of a delegate step is a SUSPENSION of this
    // generator, so it cannot move into a helper function - that is the whole shape of
    // this loop.
    //
    // The state is that one slot, and the slot lives in the scope js_scope_new makes when
    // this expression is evaluated: one per yield* per generator INSTANCE, on that
    // coroutine's own frame, nothing kept in any program-wide structure across a
    // suspension (a per-program stack would break, since `for await` suspends inside
